															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cosmic42" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1228520); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1228520&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609254245" alt="cosmic42" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1228520)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cosmic42" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1228520); return false;" >cosmic42</a></span> <span class="odate time_1505117388 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Sep 2017 08:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3589488">
									

<p>Hi, I think this is a very creative story and it has the right creepy factor where every iteration comes back more monstrous and less human. That reminds me a bit of the Xenomorph from Alien.</p>
<p>I have a question though. If we know there could be 3 new instances of graves per day, and the minimum depth is only 1 meter, why haven't we tried to dig up a fresh one? Someone should monitor the site and the moment a new one appears, get a huge Caterpillar backhoe and dig that up. Chances are good it may not have suffocated yet and a live specimen may be obtained. This could potentially halt the expansion of the cemetery, once it has successfully &quot;come back.&quot;</p>

							</div>

								
